  {
   "title": "bueno with ser / estar",
   "body": "For people, ser bueno = to be a good person or good at something; estar bueno usually means attractive or in good shape. With things, the difference is a subtler feeling that varies by dialect.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "They're good (people).",
     "es": "Son buenos."
    },
    {
     "en": "He's attractive / in good shape.",
     "es": "Está bueno."
    }
   ],
   "t": 199.5
  },
  {
   "title": "A tool English lacks",
   "body": "Ser vs estar isn't an obstacle: it's an extra way of expressing reality that you may start missing in English. You learn the nuances by listening.",
   "t": 278.2
  },
  {
   "title": "cómo está vs cómo es",
   "body": "",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I want to know how he's doing.",
     "es": "Quiero saber cómo está."
    },
    {
     "en": "I want to know what he's like.",
     "es": "Quiero saber cómo es."
    }
   ],
   "t": 351.9
  },
  {
   "title": "feliz = happy",
   "body": "As in feliz Navidad; felicity → felicidad (happiness). It works with both verbs, but you can't know that from the meaning alone; the persona safety net is always correct.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I'm happy (now).",
     "es": "Estoy feliz."
    },
    {
     "en": "I'm a happy person.",
     "es": "Soy feliz. / Soy una persona feliz."
    }
   ],
   "t": 380.1
  }
